# Ring Buffers

## What is a Ring Buffer?

A ring buffer is a fixed-size circular data structure.

```text
        ┌─────────────────────┐
        ↓                     │
[A][B][C][ ][ ][ ][ ][ ] ←────┘
```

When the end is reached, the index wraps around.

```text
0 → 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5 → 0
```

## Why Use a Ring Buffer?

A ring buffer:

* Uses fixed memory
* Avoids repeated allocation
* Has predictable memory usage
* Can provide very low overhead
* Works well for producer/consumer systems

## Core Components

Typically:

```text
buffer
head
tail
capacity
```

### Head

Points to where the producer will write.

### Tail

Points to where the consumer will read.

## Example

```text
Capacity = 5

[ A ][ B ][ C ][   ][   ]
  ↑               ↑
 tail            head
```

After consuming A:

```text
[   ][ B ][ C ][   ][   ]
      ↑
     tail
```

## Wraparound

Instead of moving beyond the end:

```c
index = (index + 1) % capacity;
```

The index wraps back to zero.

## Concurrency

There are different designs:

```text
SPSC
Single Producer / Single Consumer

MPSC
Multiple Producers / Single Consumer

MPMC
Multiple Producers / Multiple Consumers
```

SPSC is usually the simplest place to start.

## Lock-Free Ring Buffers

A more advanced implementation can use:

```text
Atomic head
+
Atomic tail
+
Memory ordering
```

Some high-performance implementations also use sequence numbers to determine whether individual slots are ready.

